    story: "Bali Tropical Trail circles the island through rice-growing landscapes, temple precincts, volcanic highlands and protected coasts. Denpasar and Sanur provide a lowland introduction before the route enters Ubud, where performance, painting and craft villages coexist with intensively managed rice terraces. The subak system is central to the story: water temples and farmer associations coordinate irrigation in a cultural landscape recognised by UNESCO. North of Ubud, the trail climbs toward Mount Batur's caldera, then reaches highland lakes and the lakeside temple of Ulun Danu Beratan. The northern stage descends through forest and waterfalls to Singaraja, once the island's colonial administrative centre. Western Bali adds mangroves and national-park habitat before the southern coast brings sea temples and limestone headlands. Sacred sites are presented with guidance on respectful dress and active ceremonies. The route also addresses pressure from tourism, water demand and waste, encouraging virtual exploration and responsible choices. Bali emerges not as a generic tropical paradise but as a living island shaped by Balinese Hindu practice, cooperative agriculture and diverse ecosystems.",
