    story: "Scandinavian Fjords & Coast follows Norway's deeply indented western shoreline from Bergen to the Lofoten Islands. Bergen begins among Bryggen's timber warehouses, a reminder that fish and seaborne trade connected the coast to northern Europe. The route enters Sognefjord country, where glaciers, waterfalls and farms occupy narrow shelves above very deep water. Flåm and Geiranger introduce famous fjord viewpoints but also the engineering of mountain roads, tunnels and ferries that keep communities connected. Ålesund adds an urban chapter rebuilt in Art Nouveau style after the 1904 fire. The Atlantic Road and Trondheim carry the journey north through exposed islands and the historic pilgrimage centre of Nidaros Cathedral. Beyond the Arctic Circle, Bodø and the Lofoten fishing villages reveal seasonal cod fisheries, sharp granite peaks and long changes in daylight. Ferry segments are celebrated as virtual crossings while users keep walking at home. The route explains that fjords are glacial valleys flooded by the sea and highlights conservation, avalanche risk and weather without turning inhabited coastal landscapes into empty wilderness.",
