    story: "Patagonia Wilderness crosses the southern Andes from Argentina's lake district to the Beagle Channel. Bariloche begins among glacial lakes and Andean-Patagonian forest, then the route moves south through broad steppe where wind and distance dominate the horizon. El Chaltén introduces the granite spires of Fitz Roy and a modern town built around trekking access. El Calafate leads to Perito Moreno Glacier, one of the most accessible outlets of the Southern Patagonian Ice Field. Crossing into Chile, Torres del Paine combines horn-shaped peaks, turquoise lakes and grasslands used by guanaco and puma. Puerto Natales adds the working history of sheep ranching and maritime supply before the trail returns to Argentina at Río Grande and Ushuaia. The final stage faces the Beagle Channel and subantarctic forests. This virtual route makes no claim that the full line is a continuous hiking trail; it links real gateways and parks while daily steps happen safely at home. Conservation, Indigenous Selk'nam and Tehuelche histories, glaciers and frontier settlement are integrated throughout.",
