    story: "Scottish Highlands & Lochs loops from Edinburgh through the western glens, islands and Great Glen. The route leaves Edinburgh's volcanic castle ridge for Stirling, where a strategic crossing guarded access between Lowlands and Highlands. Loch Lomond begins the water-and-mountain chapter before Glencoe introduces dramatic geology alongside the carefully interpreted history of the 1692 massacre. Fort William sits beneath Ben Nevis and opens the Road to the Isles. The Glenfinnan Viaduct and ferry approach to Skye combine engineering, Jacobite memory and Atlantic scenery. Portree leads into the Cuillin, Trotternish and Gaelic cultural landscape of the island. Eilean Donan Castle marks the return to the mainland, then the trail follows Loch Ness and the Caledonian Canal to Inverness. Cairngorm plateaus and Speyside close the circuit south. The route separates folklore from evidence—Nessie belongs to the legend layer—while giving Gaelic place-names, land use, clearances, conservation and living communities serious attention.",
